1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,732
Read the CTO's question once more,
and notice what isn't in it.

2
00:00:08,772 --> 00:00:14,361
No conflict. No error. No red check. The
second merge said "Merge made by the 'ort'

3
00:00:14,401 --> 00:00:17,955
strategy", listed one file,
and exited with status 0.

4
00:00:17,995 --> 00:00:22,431
main now contains the fix for a
feature that main doesn't contain.

5
00:00:22,471 --> 00:00:26,025
Keep that sentence. In step
four you watch it happen.

6
00:00:26,365 --> 00:00:29,648
You saw this once as a pointer
at the end of the merge module.

7
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:34,043
Today you get the mechanism, the root cause
in one line, and two correct ways out.

8
00:00:35,883 --> 00:00:39,389
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:44,214
In the last video you reverted ordinary
commits. A merge commit is different in one

10
00:00:44,254 --> 00:00:47,412
respect: it has two parents,
the two commits it joined.

11
00:00:47,452 --> 00:00:52,237
So "the change it made" has two meanings,
and you must tell Git which one you mean.

12
00:00:52,577 --> 00:00:56,721
That part takes five minutes. The rest
of the video is about what happens

13
00:00:56,761 --> 00:01:01,307
afterwards, because reverting a merge
changes what every later merge of the same

14
00:01:01,347 --> 00:01:05,893
branch does. You'll merge a branch, revert
the merge, try to merge it again, and

15
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:07,900
watch Git say "Already up to date".

16
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:12,291
Then you'll watch the dangerous
variant, which looks like a success.

17
00:01:12,331 --> 00:01:17,043
Then the two repairs: revert the revert,
or recreate the branch as new commits.

18
00:01:17,083 --> 00:01:22,097
And last, why a squash merge, which packs
a branch into one ordinary commit, doesn't

19
00:01:22,137 --> 00:01:23,601
have this problem at all.

20
00:01:23,941 --> 00:01:28,809
The project is a ranker for a retrieval
service, and the branch is feature/reranker.

21
00:01:30,649 --> 00:01:32,062
After this video you can:

22
00:01:32,402 --> 00:01:36,172
Revert a merge commit with -m 1
and say what that choice means.

23
00:01:36,512 --> 00:01:40,698
Explain why merging the repaired branch
again brings only the new commits.

24
00:01:41,038 --> 00:01:45,146
Repair the situation by reverting the
revert or by recreating the branch.

25
00:01:45,486 --> 00:01:47,915
Contrast the behavior
after a squash merge.

26
00:01:49,755 --> 00:01:54,193
In one sentence: git revert -m 1 <merge>
undoes the content that a merge brought

27
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:58,895
into the branch, and leaves the merge itself
in the commit graph, which changes what

28
00:01:58,935 --> 00:02:01,246
every later merge of the same branch does.

29
00:02:01,586 --> 00:02:03,101
Take the two halves separately.

30
00:02:03,441 --> 00:02:09,261
First half, the option. A merge commit
has two parents, so the inverse has to be

31
00:02:09,301 --> 00:02:15,414
computed relative to one of them. -m
<parent-number>, long form --mainline, chooses.

32
00:02:15,454 --> 00:02:21,274
On a branch that receives feature branches,
the first parent is the branch's own

33
00:02:21,314 --> 00:02:25,009
previous tip, as the video
on merge commits showed.

34
00:02:25,049 --> 00:02:30,649
So -m 1 takes out what the merge brought
in. On screen, the merge is 27bb9f5.

35
00:02:30,689 --> 00:02:36,289
Parent one is 31a221c, on main. Parent
two is c3762df, the tip of the branch.

36
00:02:36,629 --> 00:02:41,074
Second half, the consequence. The revert
commit is an ordinary commit on top of the

37
00:02:41,114 --> 00:02:45,019
merge. It changed content. It didn't
change the graph, and no commit can.

38
00:02:45,059 --> 00:02:49,504
The merge commit is still an ancestor of
main, and through its second parent so are

39
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:54,043
all the commits of the feature branch. Git's
how-to on this situation says the same:

40
00:02:54,083 --> 00:02:58,150
a revert undoes the data a merge brought,
and none of its effect on history.

41
00:02:58,490 --> 00:03:03,684
Now recall what a merge is: it combines what
each side changed since the merge base,

42
00:03:03,724 --> 00:03:08,793
the best common ancestor of the two sides.
The branch has now gained one commit, a

43
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:13,778
fix. Try it now, thirty seconds: point at
the merge base of main and the branch.

44
00:03:13,818 --> 00:03:15,024
Say its ID out loud.

45
00:03:19,864 --> 00:03:25,122
It's c3762df. After the first merge, the
merge base of main and the branch is the

46
00:03:25,162 --> 00:03:30,420
old tip of the branch. So at the next
merge, only commits after that tip count as

47
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:35,325
the branch's change. On main's side,
the revert is a change since the base.

48
00:03:35,365 --> 00:03:39,053
It deletes the feature, nothing
opposes it, and it stays.

49
00:03:39,393 --> 00:03:42,601
The manual states the rule bluntly: reverting
a merge "declares that you will never

50
00:03:42,641 --> 00:03:44,440
want the tree changes
brought in by the merge".

51
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:48,270
There are two ways out. Way one:
revert the revert, and then merge.

52
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:52,590
Way two: recreate the branch as new commits,
which aren't ancestors of main, so an

53
00:03:52,630 --> 00:03:54,382
ordinary merge brings all of them.

54
00:03:54,722 --> 00:03:59,445
When not to revert a merge at all? The
how-to advises finding and reverting the

55
00:03:59,485 --> 00:04:04,390
single faulty commit, or fixing forward,
and reverting a whole merge only when the

56
00:04:04,430 --> 00:04:07,585
merge as such was the mistake.
There's a second cost.

57
00:04:07,625 --> 00:04:12,590
For git bisect, the binary search over
commits, the revert is one large commit that

58
00:04:12,630 --> 00:04:16,509
undoes many small ones, and so is
the later revert of the revert.

59
00:04:18,349 --> 00:04:23,062
Go back to the ledger from the last video.
A merge posted a whole batch of entries

60
00:04:23,102 --> 00:04:26,249
from another department, with
a note: "batch received".

61
00:04:26,289 --> 00:04:29,205
The revert posts one reversing
entry for the batch.

62
00:04:29,245 --> 00:04:32,451
The books now balance as if
the batch had never arrived.

63
00:04:32,491 --> 00:04:37,203
But the note "batch received" is still
there, and the clerk who processes the next

64
00:04:37,243 --> 00:04:40,159
delivery from that department
reads the note first.

65
00:04:40,199 --> 00:04:43,347
Everything listed on it is
skipped as already received.

66
00:04:43,687 --> 00:04:48,130
Where the picture breaks: a clerk could
be told "that batch was reversed, take it

67
00:04:48,170 --> 00:04:51,729
again". Git can't be told. Merge
reads the graph, not intentions.

68
00:04:51,769 --> 00:04:56,379
The only ways to say it are in the graph's
own language: post an entry that reverses

69
00:04:56,419 --> 00:05:00,088
the reversal, or deliver the same
goods under new delivery numbers.

70
00:05:01,928 --> 00:05:07,629
Read it left to right along main: the
fork, the first merge 27bb9f5, the revert

71
00:05:07,669 --> 00:05:12,861
7e5a38e, the revert of the revert
4b3560c, and the second merge 018849f.

72
00:05:13,201 --> 00:05:18,381
Put your finger on c3762df. After the first
merge it's an ancestor of main, and it

73
00:05:18,421 --> 00:05:23,665
stays one whatever is committed afterwards.
The second merge can only bring what is

74
00:05:23,705 --> 00:05:26,338
to the right of it on the branch: 6b4e788.

75
00:05:28,178 --> 00:05:28,538
Step 1: the merge.

76
00:05:28,484 --> 00:05:34,185
A true merge, 27bb9f5, two branch
commits. rerank.py is in the listing.

77
00:05:34,525 --> 00:05:35,182
Step 2: revert it. git revert 🟡 CAUTION.

78
00:05:35,522 --> 00:05:40,769
Without -m, Git refuses: the commit is
a merge but no -m option was given.

79
00:05:40,809 --> 00:05:46,342
The parents are 31a221c, the previous
tip of main, and c3762df, the tip of the

80
00:05:46,382 --> 00:05:51,701
branch. With -m 1 the revert goes
through, and rerank.py is gone from main.

81
00:05:52,041 --> 00:05:57,614
The generated message records which parent
was treated as the mainline: "reversing

82
00:05:57,654 --> 00:06:02,816
changes made to", followed by the ID of
parent 1. And the graph: the revert,

83
00:06:02,856 --> 00:06:06,923
7e5a38e, sits on top of the
merge. The merge is still there.

84
00:06:07,263 --> 00:06:12,566
Step 3: merge the branch again.
Nothing has changed on the branch.

85
00:06:12,606 --> 00:06:17,908
Predict the output of git merge
feature/reranker. Say it out loud.

86
00:06:17,948 --> 00:06:18,717
I'll wait.

87
00:06:23,557 --> 00:06:28,515
"Already up to date." And the two IDs below
it are the same: the merge base of main

88
00:06:28,555 --> 00:06:32,851
and the branch is the branch tip. For
Git, the branch is already merged.

89
00:06:33,191 --> 00:06:40,440
Step 4: the dangerous variant. The team
fixes the bug with one more commit on the

90
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:46,110
branch, and merges. Quick quiz:
what does main have afterwards?

91
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:50,700
A, the whole feature plus
the fix. B, only the fix.

92
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:53,130
C, a conflict. Your answer?

93
00:06:57,970 --> 00:07:03,410
B, only the fix. No conflict, no warning.
One file changed: serve.yaml gained

94
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:08,108
rerank_top_k: 50. There's no
rerank.py, and rank.py has no import.

95
00:07:08,148 --> 00:07:12,022
main received the fix for a
feature it doesn't contain.

96
00:07:12,062 --> 00:07:16,862
That's the sentence from the start
of the video, on your own screen.

97
00:07:17,202 --> 00:07:21,466
Here's the mechanism as a root-cause box.
The revert removed the content of the

98
00:07:21,506 --> 00:07:25,716
merge and left the merge in the graph.
The fix is one of two ways out, and the

99
00:07:25,756 --> 00:07:30,292
prevention is to write the re-merge procedure
into the message of the revert commit.

100
00:07:30,632 --> 00:07:35,883
Step 5: way out 1, revert the revert. The
demo first takes the half-working merge

101
00:07:35,923 --> 00:07:41,370
away with git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD 🔴
DANGEROUS. The five answers, briefly, because

102
00:07:41,410 --> 00:07:46,791
you met them two videos ago. It moves the
branch and rewrites the index and tracked

103
00:07:46,831 --> 00:07:52,147
files. It destroys uncommitted changes.
Preview with git status -s, and by reading

104
00:07:52,187 --> 00:07:56,001
what ORIG_HEAD names. Commits
come back through the reflog.

105
00:07:56,041 --> 00:08:01,357
And it's appropriate here because the tree
is clean, the merge is unpublished, and

106
00:08:01,397 --> 00:08:04,166
it's the very next
command after the merge.

107
00:08:04,506 --> 00:08:09,934
git revert 7e5a38e reverts the revert and
brings the content of the first merge

108
00:08:09,974 --> 00:08:15,609
back. Git 2.55 names such a commit Reapply
"...". Then the merge adds the fix, and

109
00:08:15,649 --> 00:08:17,547
rerank.py is in the listing.

110
00:08:17,887 --> 00:08:22,854
This is the graph of today's diagram.
Merge, revert, reapply, second merge.

111
00:08:22,894 --> 00:08:24,990
And rank.py has its import line.

112
00:08:25,330 --> 00:08:30,579
Step 6: way out 2, recreate the branch.
labs/run ch11/revert-merge-rebuild.

113
00:08:30,619 --> 00:08:36,291
The idea: new commits aren't ancestors of
main. The obvious command is git rebase

114
00:08:36,331 --> 00:08:40,945
main, which replays a branch's
commits as new ones on top of main.

115
00:08:40,985 --> 00:08:45,247
Predict how many commits it
replays. Say the number out loud.

116
00:08:50,087 --> 00:08:55,113
One. A plain rebase replays only what
main lacks, and main lacks only the fix.

117
00:08:55,153 --> 00:08:59,790
The feature is still missing. If you
said three, you're in good company.

118
00:09:00,130 --> 00:09:05,319
What works is git rebase --no-ff 🟡 from
the commit where the branch started, an

119
00:09:05,359 --> 00:09:08,629
option the manual documents
for exactly this case.

120
00:09:16,969 --> 00:09:20,208
Where did the branch start? At the merge
base of the two parents of the reverted

121
00:09:20,248 --> 00:09:22,503
merge: fbb8230. "Rebase forced",
three commits replayed.

122
00:09:22,543 --> 00:09:25,741
In the graph the three commits have new
IDs and hang off fbb8230 beside the old

123
00:09:25,781 --> 00:09:25,946
ones.

124
00:09:26,286 --> 00:09:30,161
An ordinary merge now brings
rerank.py, the import and the fix.

125
00:09:30,201 --> 00:09:35,257
The price is a rewritten feature branch,
which is the subject of the rebase module

126
00:09:35,297 --> 00:09:36,997
that starts in three videos.

127
00:09:37,337 --> 00:09:42,171
Step 7: the squash contrast.
labs/run ch11/revert-squash-remerge.

128
00:09:42,211 --> 00:09:48,245
The branch went in as one ordinary commit,
made with git merge --squash, and that

129
00:09:48,285 --> 00:09:49,745
commit was reverted.

130
00:09:50,085 --> 00:09:53,350
No -m was needed: the squashed
commit has one parent.

131
00:09:53,390 --> 00:09:57,403
Now merge the branch, with its
fix. Predict the merge base first.

132
00:10:05,243 --> 00:10:09,440
The merge base is still fbb8230,
where the branch started.

133
00:10:09,780 --> 00:10:15,052
There's no second parent, the branch commits
never became ancestors of main, and so

134
00:10:15,092 --> 00:10:20,236
the merge brings the whole branch: three
files. Recall the squash trap from video

135
00:10:20,276 --> 00:10:23,884
36: content without ancestry.
There it caused a conflict.

136
00:10:23,924 --> 00:10:27,020
Here the same property is
why the re-merge works.

137
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:30,508
Five mistakes to watch for.

138
00:10:30,848 --> 00:10:34,325
Merging the repaired branch again
and trusting a clean result.

139
00:10:34,365 --> 00:10:38,921
Root cause: the revert removed the content
of the merge and left the merge in the

140
00:10:38,961 --> 00:10:43,289
graph, so the merge base is the old
branch tip and only newer commits arrive.

141
00:10:43,629 --> 00:10:47,878
Running git rebase main on the branch
to "refresh" it before re-merging.

142
00:10:47,918 --> 00:10:52,286
Root cause: a plain rebase replays only
commits that main does not have as

143
00:10:52,326 --> 00:10:55,384
ancestors, and the old branch
commits are ancestors.

144
00:10:55,724 --> 00:11:00,351
Choosing the parent number by guessing.
Root cause: -m selects the parent that

145
00:11:00,391 --> 00:11:05,257
counts as the mainline; on a branch that
receives merges that is parent 1, and the

146
00:11:05,297 --> 00:11:07,770
other number computes a different inverse.

147
00:11:08,110 --> 00:11:11,588
Reverting a whole merge because
one commit in it was faulty.

148
00:11:11,628 --> 00:11:16,395
Root cause: the revert, and later the revert
of the revert, are large commits that

149
00:11:16,435 --> 00:11:18,506
hide the small ones from git bisect.

150
00:11:18,846 --> 00:11:23,915
Assuming the same problem after a squash
merge. Root cause: a squash commit has one

151
00:11:23,955 --> 00:11:28,039
parent, so no ancestry was recorded
and the merge base never moved.

152
00:11:29,879 --> 00:11:34,991
Now, out of the lab. A search team merges
a cross-encoder reranker on Tuesday.

153
00:11:35,031 --> 00:11:40,473
Latency doubles. On Wednesday the on-call
engineer reverts the merge with -m 1, and

154
00:11:40,513 --> 00:11:43,182
production recovers. That part goes well.

155
00:11:43,522 --> 00:11:48,552
The following Monday the branch has one
more commit, a cap on reranker candidates.

156
00:11:48,592 --> 00:11:51,890
Someone opens a new pull
request from the same branch.

157
00:11:51,930 --> 00:11:55,662
The diff shows one line. Reviewers
approve a one-line change.

158
00:11:55,702 --> 00:11:59,804
It merges cleanly, and the release
notes say "reranker re-enabled".

159
00:11:59,844 --> 00:12:03,452
It isn't. And nobody was careless:
every step looked right.

160
00:12:03,792 --> 00:12:08,195
The prevention from the root-cause box
costs one paragraph: write the re-merge

161
00:12:08,235 --> 00:12:12,923
procedure into the message of the revert
commit. "Before merging this branch again,

162
00:12:12,963 --> 00:12:17,594
revert this commit first." The person who
needs that sentence will be reading that

163
00:12:17,634 --> 00:12:17,992
commit.

164
00:12:18,332 --> 00:12:22,995
On GitHub, the Revert button on a merged
pull request "creates a new pull request

165
00:12:23,035 --> 00:12:27,640
that reverts the original merge commit",
and gh pr revert <number> does the same

166
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:32,168
from the command line. This is described
from the documentation, not run here.

167
00:12:32,208 --> 00:12:37,045
The page says nothing about a later re-merge.
By the mechanics you have seen, a pull

168
00:12:37,085 --> 00:12:41,806
request that was merged with a merge commit
and then reverted needs one of the two

169
00:12:41,846 --> 00:12:44,303
ways out before its
branch is merged again.

170
00:12:46,143 --> 00:12:52,366
Your turn. Do Lab 8.3, "Revert a merge,
then re-merge", in lab-manual/m08-undo.md.

171
00:12:52,706 --> 00:12:54,590
Predict at three points, in writing:

172
00:12:54,930 --> 00:12:59,710
Before the revert: what are the two parents
of the merge, and what will the tree

173
00:12:59,750 --> 00:13:00,976
look like after -m 1?

174
00:13:01,316 --> 00:13:06,071
Before the second merge: what does git
merge-base main <branch> print, and therefore

175
00:13:06,111 --> 00:13:08,069
which commits will the merge bring?

176
00:13:08,409 --> 00:13:13,401
Before each repair: what will the graph look
like afterwards, and which IDs, if any,

177
00:13:13,441 --> 00:13:14,120
will be new?

178
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:20,783
The challenge is Exercise 8.10, Level 5,
"a feature that was merged twice and is

179
00:13:20,823 --> 00:13:24,839
half missing", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

180
00:13:26,679 --> 00:13:31,796
Q182: "You reverted a merge with -m 1 last
week. Today the fixed branch was merged

181
00:13:31,836 --> 00:13:36,953
again and the feature is incomplete. Explain
the mechanism with the merge base and

182
00:13:36,993 --> 00:13:41,921
give two correct procedures. What would
-m 2 have undone, and why does a squash

183
00:13:41,961 --> 00:13:43,682
merge not show the problem?"

184
00:13:46,022 --> 00:13:49,116
Answer out loud first. This
is a four-part question.

185
00:13:49,156 --> 00:13:54,118
A strong answer takes the parts in order,
and keeps one idea running through all of

186
00:13:54,158 --> 00:13:58,819
them: content against ancestry. It states
where the merge base is and why, and

187
00:13:58,859 --> 00:14:03,881
derives the missing half from that. It gives
both procedures, with the cost of each.

188
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:08,703
For -m 2 it reasons from what "relative
to that parent" means and doesn't guess.

189
00:14:08,743 --> 00:14:12,138
And for the squash it says
what is absent from the graph.

190
00:14:12,178 --> 00:14:14,006
Draw the graph while you speak.

191
00:14:15,846 --> 00:14:18,773
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

192
00:14:19,113 --> 00:14:24,185
A merge commit has two parents, so git
revert needs -m to know which one is the

193
00:14:24,225 --> 00:14:27,744
mainline, and -m 1 takes out
what the merge brought in.

194
00:14:27,784 --> 00:14:32,986
The revert changes content and leaves the
merge in the graph, so the branch's old

195
00:14:33,026 --> 00:14:37,904
commits stay ancestors of main, and a
later merge brings only newer commits.

196
00:14:38,244 --> 00:14:43,419
The two repairs are to revert the revert
and then merge, or to recreate the branch

197
00:14:43,459 --> 00:14:47,299
as new commits with git rebase
--no-ff from where it started.

198
00:14:47,639 --> 00:14:52,441
After a squash merge no ancestry was
recorded, so a later merge brings the whole

199
00:14:52,481 --> 00:14:56,860
branch. Where possible I revert the
one faulty commit and keep the merge.

200
00:14:58,700 --> 00:15:06,925
Read section 11.9 of Chapter 11.

201
00:15:07,265 --> 00:15:12,402
A clean merge that loses half a feature is
no longer a mystery to you: you can point

202
00:15:12,442 --> 00:15:17,332
at the merge base and say why. Do the lab,
and write your three predictions down

203
00:15:17,372 --> 00:15:21,954
first. Next time: git clean, and stash,
uncommitted work parked as commits.

204
00:15:21,994 --> 00:15:25,159
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

205
00:15:25,199 --> 00:15:26,638
See you in the next one.
